Q: Initialization of all elements of a multidimensional std::array to one default value A: <p>Multidimensional contiguous array is a way more complicated concept than meets the eye that's why C++ is still struggling with standardizing a certain interface for it. The fixed sized <code>std::array</code> provides the luxury of <em>nested array</em> definition as illusrtated in the OP. In contrast, the dynamic array <code>std::vector</code>should not be jagged/nested as in OP. C++23 provides <code>std::md_span</code>, but prefer C++20 <code>&lt;ranges&gt;</code> for the task:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;ranges&gt;
auto constexpr d_min = 2z,
               d_mid = 5z,
               d_max = 2z;

std::vector vec // 20 × 2.0
(d_max * d_mid * d_max, 2.0d);

std::ranges::fill(vec, -1);

//as if: double (&amp;vec3)[d_max][d_mid][d_min] = vec;

//Requires std=c++23
auto vec3d
= vec                      //[max * mid * min]
| std::views::chunk(d_min) //[max * mid] [min]
| std::views::chunk(d_mid);//[max] [mid] [min]
</code></pre>
<p>This technique can work with <code>std::array</code> or C++26 <code>std::inplace_vector</code> as well. But <code>vec3d</code> will be a <em>view(≈reference)</em> to the original object, and should not be returned from creating scope of the original object - to avoid dangling.</p>
<p>With nesting approach however, <code>std::array</code> can benefit from <code>&lt;ranges&gt;</code> a different way too:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>using a_min = std::array&lt;double, d_min&gt;;
using a_mid = std::array&lt;a_min, d_mid&gt;;
using a_max = std::array&lt;a_mid, d_max&gt;;

a_max arr3d {};

auto arr_flat
= arr3d      v v v //[max] [mid] [min]
| std::views::join //[max * mid] [min]
| std::views::join;//[max * mid * min]

std::ranges::fill(arr_flat, -1);
</code></pre>
<p>Again <code>arr_flat</code> is <em>view</em>, and shouldn't be returned from creation scope of <code>arr</code>. It would be best if I had sent the result of second <code>join</code> directly to <code>fill</code>, but I introduced the temporary view <code>arr_flat</code> for illustration purpose.</p>
<p>I hope you can upgrade to <code>std=c++23</code>, because it makes code a lot easier to maintain, and read.</p>

Q: Null is not an object for DOM A: <p>a simple version of the working code</p>
<pre><code>// 5 empty arrays for storing user input data
const tasksList1 = []; const tasksList2 = []; const tasksList3 = []; const tasksList4 =[]; const tasksList5 =[]

// storage array
var tasks = [ tasksList1, tasksList2, tasksList3, tasksList4, tasksList5 ];

// array of links to &lt;ul&gt; elements
const UL_LIST = [ document.getElementById(&quot;list1&quot;), 
    document.getElementById(&quot;list2&quot;), 
    document.getElementById(&quot;list3&quot;), 
    document.getElementById(&quot;list4&quot;), 
    document.getElementById(&quot;list5&quot;) ];

// link to the input field
const INPUT = document.getElementById('taskInput');
// to the list selection item
const SELECTOR = document.getElementById('listSelector');

// Function to display the list
// redraws all the lists on the page.
function displayTasks() {
// Loop through all the &quot;tasks&quot; lists
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; tasks.length; i++) {
    let html = &quot;&quot;;
    for (let j = 0; j &lt; tasks[i].length; j++) {
// redraws only &lt;li&gt; elements
        html += &quot;&lt;li&gt;&quot; + tasks[i][j] + &quot; &lt;button onclick='removeTask(&quot; + i + &quot;,&quot; + j + &quot;)'&gt;x&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&quot;;
    }
    UL_LIST[i].innerHTML = html;
  }

}


function addTask() {
  const text = INPUT.value.trim();

  if (text === '') return;
// determining the key of the list. The last character of the index
  const targetListId = parseInt(SELECTOR.value.slice(-1)) - 1;

  if (targetListId &lt; 0) return;

  INPUT.value = '';
  tasks[targetListId].push(text);
  saveTasks();
}

function removeTask(i,j) {
  tasks[i].splice(j, 1);
  saveTasks();
}

function saveTasks() {
  localStorage.setItem(&quot;tasks&quot;, JSON.stringify(tasks));
  displayTasks();
}

function loadTasks() {

  if (localStorage.getItem(&quot;tasks&quot;) !== null) {
    tasks = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(&quot;tasks&quot;));
  }

  displayTasks();
}

/* !!! */saveTasks(); // just clearing the storage. Delete this line after the first run

loadTasks();
</code></pre>
<p>comments only for modified elements. The rest is left by the author.</p>

Q: How To Make An Animate Menu Icon Show/Hide Menu A: <p>fix it in CSS</p>
<pre><code>.menuIcon {
    display: inline-block;
    cursor: pointer;
    float: left;
    margin-top: 1.3em;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    margin-left: 1em;
    margin-right: 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>It's all code in JS.</p>
<pre><code>function myFunction(x) {
  let menuIcon = document.getElementById(&quot;menuIcon&quot;) ;
  menuIcon.classList.toggle(&quot;change&quot;);
  if(menuIcon.classList.contains('change')){
    document.getElementById(&quot;mySidebar&quot;).style.width = &quot;250px&quot;;    
  }
  else {
    document.getElementById(&quot;mySidebar&quot;).style.width = 0;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>It's all code in HTML</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
    &lt;html&gt;
    &lt;head&gt;
      &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt;
     &lt;/head&gt;

    &lt;body&gt;

      &lt;div class=&quot;menuIcon&quot; id=&quot;menuIcon&quot; onclick=&quot;myFunction()&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;menuBar1&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;menuBar2&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;menuBar3&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt; 

      &lt;div id=&quot;mySidebar&quot; class=&quot;sidebar&quot;&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;javascript:void(0)&quot; class=&quot;closeBtn&quot; onclick=&quot;myFunction()&quot;&gt;×&lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Services&lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Clients&lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;    

    &lt;/body&gt;
    &lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Error &#39;UNABLE_TO_GET_ISSUER_CERT_LOCALLY&#39; A: <p>This error can come when we are calling a https from a local system.</p>
<p><strong>For Nest.JS application</strong></p>
<p>Go to your, app.module.ts, add the code for HttpModule as shown below</p>
<pre><code>import { HttpModule } from &quot;@nestjs/axios&quot;;

@Module({
  imports: HttpModule.register({
    // Create an HTTPS agent to ignore SSL certificate errors
    httpsAgent: new (require('https').Agent)({
      rejectUnauthorized: false,
    })})],
  controllers: [..........],
  providers: [.........],
})
export class AppModule {}
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> it's safe to have environment variable check for this rejectUnauthorised:</p>
</blockquote>
<pre><code>rejectUnauthorized: process.env.NODE_ENV == &quot;dev&quot; ? false : true
</code></pre>
